F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb7%2Fad%2Fecff75644e249578576028649d75%2F2026-07-evanstonreparations-cchambers-040.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2531+0+235/resize/900/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb7%2Fad%2Fecff75644e249578576028649d75%2F2026-07-evanstonreparations-cchambers-040.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2531+0+235/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb7%2Fad%2Fecff75644e249578576028649d75%2F2026-07-evanstonreparations-cchambers-040.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2531+0+235/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb7%2Fad%2Fecff75644e249578576028649d75%2F2026-07-evanstonreparations-cchambers-040.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2531+0+235/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb7%2Fad%2Fecff75644e249578576028649d75%2F2026-07-evanstonreparations-cchambers-040.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2531+0+235/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb7%2Fad%2Fecff75644e249578576028649d75%2F2026-07-evanstonreparations-cchambers-040.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2531+0+235/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb7%2Fad%2Fecff75644e249578576028649d75%2F2026-07-evanstonreparations-cchambers-040.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2531+0+235/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb7%2Fad%2Fecff75644e249578576028649d75%2F2026-07-evanstonreparations-cchambers-040.jpg" class="img" alt="Reparations recipient Kimberly Holmes-Murphy sits outside her home in Evanston, Ill. Holmes-Murphy is a grantee of the city’s Local Reparations Restorative Housing Program designed to compensate Black residents for decades of systemic discrimination." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Reparations recipient Kimberly Holmes-Murphy sits outside her home in Evanston, Ill. Holmes-Murphy is a grantee of the city's Local Reparations Restorative Housing Program designed to compensate Black residents for decades of systemic discrimination.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Candace Dane Chambers for NPR                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;b class="toggle-caption"&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/b&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;The Trump administration's Justice Department is investigating Evanston, Ill., accusing it of racial discrimination. Evanston is the first U.S. city to implement a reparations program to compensate Black residents for historical discrimination. The program has provided more than 300 individuals with $25,000 grants to compensate for past housing segregation. The Justice Department is joining a lawsuit filed by a conservative group that alleges the city's program is racially discriminatory, as it only qualifies Black people for assistance. Here's what we know &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/14/nx-s1-5869793/evanston-illinois-reparations-trump-administration" target="_blank"&gt;about what is happening&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🏠 In 2019, a councilmember researching the decline of the city's Black population discovered a link between affordability issues faced by Black families and a 1921 zoning law that confined them to a single neighborhood. This law allowed local banks to deny mortgages, hindering wealth-building for generations.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🏠 The City Council recognized the harm, apologized and committed $20 million from the city's tax on cannabis sales and real estate transfers to pay reparations.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🏠 Applicants were required to be Black and demonstrate that they or an ancestor had lived in Evanston from 1919 to 1969, during the height of housing segregation.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🏠 The Justice Department claims that Evanston is infringing upon the Constitution's equal protection clause, which prohibits government discrimination based on race.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;3 things to know before you go&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-106892" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 3000;         --source-height: 1688;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/400/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/900/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+50/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1f%2Ffb%2Fd69cf7ce4d239239448ab31f413d%2Fgettyimages-83685976.jpg" class="img" alt="Visionary costume and fashion designer Bob Mackie was known as the “Sultan of Sequins” for his eye-catching celebrity looks. He&amp;#039;s pictured above in 2008." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Visionary costume and fashion designer Bob Mackie was known as the "Sultan of Sequins" for his eye-catching celebrity looks. He's pictured above in 2008.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Toby Canham/Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;b class="toggle-caption"&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/b&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ol class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;Prolific costume and fashion designer Bob Mackie &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/14/nx-s1-5640705/bob-mackie-dead-cher-marilyn-monroe-elton-john-fashion" target="_blank"&gt;died of pneumonia&lt;/a&gt; in Palm Springs, Calif., on Monday. He was 87.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Mitch McConnell (R-Ky.) &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/14/nx-s1-5968909/mitch-mcconnell-senate-return" target="_blank"&gt;returned to the Senate&lt;/a&gt; yesterday after a months-long medical absence stemming from a fall.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;The Trump administration is &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/14/nx-s1-5968785/epa-repeals-pollution-limits-on-power-plants" target="_blank"&gt;repealing limits on greenhouse gas emissions&lt;/a&gt; from fossil fuel-fired power plants. The move eliminates the Environmental Protection Agency rules from the Biden administration that required existing coal and new natural gas plants to cut carbon dioxide pollution.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This newsletter was edited by &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/people/859721331/suzanne-nuyen"target="_blank"   &gt;&lt;em&gt;Suzanne Nuyen&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;</description>
